<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

    <!-- GY-521 breakout (MPU6050) on the ESP32's I2C bus, read through the
         base controller's `i` command by the DiffDriveSerial hardware
         interface and published by imu_sensor_broadcaster as /imu_broad/imu
         in THIS frame. Added 18 Sep 2026 (D-25).

         THIS FILE IS THE IMU EXTRINSICS, and the rpy is the part that
         matters. The hardware interface publishes angular velocity and
         acceleration in the chip's RAW axes, unrotated; robot_localization
         looks up imu_link -> base_link from TF and rotates them itself. So
         the mounting orientation lives here, once, and nowhere in code -
         the same principle D-10 applied to the camera.

         A wrong sign on the yaw axis does not look wrong: the EKF simply
         weights a gyro that turns the opposite way to the wheels, heading
         drifts at roughly twice the true turn rate, and slam_toolbox's
         +/-10 deg matcher window is exhausted in the first corner. It reads
         as "the EKF made things worse", not as a sign error.

         MEASURE IT. Do not read it off the silkscreen:

             ros2 run my_bot imu_check.py  (its axes flag; stack DOWN, robot in hand)

         which turns/tilts the robot by hand, identifies which raw axis is
         body x, y and z with sign, checks the result is a proper rotation,
         and prints the rpy line for the joint below.

         Position matters far less than orientation for what the EKF uses:
         angular velocity is the same everywhere on a rigid body, and the
         accelerometer is not fused (D-25). The lever arm only enters if
         linear acceleration is ever fused, so a tape measure is enough. -->

    <!-- ===== ORIENTATION MEASURED 18 Sep 2026; POSITION STILL A TAPE JOB ===
         The rpy below is MEASURED, and reads 0 0 0 because the chip really is
         mounted with its axes along the body's. That the value did not change
         is not the same as the value being unconfirmed: before 18 Sep it was
         an assumption about a silkscreen, and now it is a measurement. See
         the joint.

         The xyz is still a placeholder (0, 0, 0.10) and deliberately not
         chased: angular velocity is identical at every point of a rigid body
         and the accelerometer is not fused (D-25), so the lever arm enters
         nothing the EKF computes. It affects only where the box is drawn in
         RViz. Tape it if that ever matters. -->

    <!-- Height of the chip above the FLOOR, and distance from the drive axle
         POSITIVE FORWARD, in the same terms lidar.xacro and camera.xacro use
         because those are what a tape measure returns. -->
    <xacro:property name="imu_height_above_ground" value="0.10"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_offset_from_axle" value="0.0"/>
    <!-- Lateral offset, POSITIVE LEFT (REP-103). -->
    <xacro:property name="imu_offset_y" value="0.0"/>

    <xacro:property name="imu_offset_x" value="${wheel_offset_x + imu_offset_from_axle}"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_offset_z" value="${wheel_offset_z + imu_height_above_ground - wheel_radius}"/>

    <joint name="imu_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="chassis"/>
        <child link="imu_link"/>
        <!-- rpy: raw chip axes expressed in the body frame.

             MEASURED 2026-09-18 with `imu_check.py` and its axes flag: the
             robot was turned CCW by hand, then tipped nose-down, then
             left-side-down, and the raw axis that responded to each was
             recorded with its sign. Result: body +x = raw +x, +y = raw +y,
             +z = raw +z: identity, det +1, a proper rotation. So the chip
             is mounted x-forward, y-left, z-up, and the 0 0 0 below is a
             result rather than the assumption it was before that run.

             Re-run the same check after ANY remount. A wrong yaw sign here
             is invisible in TF and in RViz and makes the EKF worse than no
             EKF: it weights the gyro 1000:1 over the wheels, so heading
             would drift at roughly twice the true turn rate and exhaust
             slam_toolbox's +/-10 deg window in the first corner. -->
        <origin xyz="${imu_offset_x} ${imu_offset_y} ${imu_offset_z}"
                rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="imu_link">
        <visual>
            <geometry>
                <box size="0.021 0.016 0.003"/>
            </geometry>
            <material name="black"/>
        </visual>
        <!-- No collision, no inertial: a 2 g breakout on a header. -->
    </link>

</robot>
